decision rather than on the evidence.

## The facts artefact — f1-facts.db, CC BY 4.0

f1-facts.db is f1.db less everything that stays share-alike. It is published
as a release asset beside f1.db and f1-geometry.db, from the first release
after v2.25, and it is offered whole
under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License
(CC BY 4.0), with no share-alike:

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

What it holds: the rows that cite F1DB by Marcel Overdijk, published under
CC BY 4.0 — among them the races, the full classification, qualifying,
practice, the sprints, standings, pit stops, the driver, constructor,
chassis, engine and entrant registers, and F1DB's circuit outlines; the rows
that cite an official source for a fact — the FIA, formula1.com, a team, a
circuit or a driver — which hold the fact and none of the source's prose;
and this project's own writing granted CC BY 4.0 above.

What it leaves out, and why — everything here that would carry a
share-alike, or that this project has not granted:

  - What was taken from Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0): the car specifications
    and design histories, the circuit layouts, the claims and driver note
    sources that cite an article, and the six team radio quotations.
  - The prose fields that stay CC BY-SA 4.0, some of them following a
    Wikipedia article: the notes and descriptions on circuits, drivers,
    constructors, seasons, races, regulations and qualifying formats, which
    the prose pass measures and neither grant above reaches.
  - The register of Wikimedia Commons photographs, each of which carries
    its own licence, several of them share-alike.
  - This project's own writing that neither grant above reaches: the
    glossary, `technical_innovations`, and the source registry's account
    of each source, among others.
  - The values a recorded disagreement compares, and its status note: the
    values come from whichever sources it compares, often an article, and
    the note is not among the writing granted above. Its assessment is
    carried, wherever the column it is about is carried.
  - The circuit centrelines, which are ODbL and never in f1.db either, and
    the four tables of FOM-owned timing, which are empty in f1.db too.

The race rows f1.db takes from Wikipedia's season articles are not left
out: they are rebuilt from F1DB, so that nothing Wikipedia made is in the
file, and some of its values therefore differ from f1.db's. Its source
registry gives this project's own source no share-alike, since the only
writing of this project's it holds is what the grants above cover. The
file's own `meta` table states its terms and says where it differs, and
tools/facts_artefact.py in the repository names every table and column it
leaves out, with the reason for each. ATTRIBUTION.md gives the credit it
carries.

f1.db itself stays CC BY-SA 4.0 as above. Nothing share-alike is in
f1-facts.db, so taking it takes none of f1.db's share-alike with it.

Inside f1.db, the `column_origin` table says where each column's values come
from, as far as tools/facts_artefact.py's declaration establishes it:
another column of the same row, the claims, this project's text that the
prose pass labels against Wikipedia, this project's own record (a citation,
a confidence tier, an identifier, a state, a classification, a link, its
own writing outside the prose pass, or its account of itself), a figure or
record build.py computes from other rows, or not established. It records
origins only. The terms a source's values come under are the source
registry's; the table grants nothing, and changes nothing above. It is not
in f1-facts.db, some of whose columns are rebuilt or left out.

